WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.851 --> 00:00:02.883
You are the consultant at a client.

2
00:00:03.621 --> 00:00:07.336
First the scope: what your project will deliver, and what it won't.

3
00:00:08.461 --> 00:00:12.443
Then the goal sentence: one line with what the project must achieve.

4
00:00:13.122 --> 00:00:16.617
For example: jobs cancelled in the week before they are due,

5
00:00:16.733 --> 00:00:19.473
from 18 to under 8 percent, by June.

6
00:00:20.484 --> 00:00:22.527
Test every request against it.

7
00:00:23.927 --> 00:00:26.992
At a cable manufacturer you improve the weekly planning,

8
00:00:27.143 --> 00:00:30.951
planned at 40 consultant days: the time the client pays for.

9
00:00:31.942 --> 00:00:33.649
Can you also look at line four?

10
00:00:34.419 --> 00:00:38.099
Could you join the meeting about the new ERP, the system for orders,

11
00:00:38.227 --> 00:00:39.191
stock and planning?

12
00:00:39.887 --> 00:00:42.244
Each request is small and reasonable.

13
00:00:43.273 --> 00:00:47.940
By week five you've said yes to five: a report for sales, a stock check,

14
00:00:48.149 --> 00:00:52.027
a session with the ERP team, and two extra analyses.

15
00:00:52.738 --> 00:00:54.178
Each about 2 days.

16
00:00:55.245 --> 00:01:00.307
Together 10 days, 25 percent extra, and the plan still shows green.

17
00:01:01.035 --> 00:01:04.344
That is scope creep: growth nobody formally agreed.

18
00:01:05.406 --> 00:01:09.063
Signs: new people at your meetings, the goal sentence shifting,

19
00:01:09.261 --> 00:01:12.605
and you working evenings while the plan says you're on track.

20
00:01:13.938 --> 00:01:18.547
So make each request visible: what, by whom, why, and what it does to time,

21
00:01:18.756 --> 00:01:20.172
cost, risk and the goal.

22
00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:23.213
Small things go on a weekly list.

23
00:01:23.988 --> 00:01:27.691
Bigger ones become a change request: written, with its impact,

24
00:01:27.866 --> 00:01:29.387
and the sponsor decides.

25
00:01:30.471 --> 00:01:32.735
Say: yes, and here is the cost.

26
00:01:33.501 --> 00:01:38.609
We can add line four, for about three weeks extra, or we drop the shift handover.

27
00:01:39.325 --> 00:01:40.323
Which do you prefer?

28
00:01:41.347 --> 00:01:44.912
Not a no, but the trade-off: what you give up to get something else.

29
00:01:45.612 --> 00:01:48.619
Most requests shrink or disappear once priced.

30
00:01:49.646 --> 00:01:53.976
And never decide alone on scope: it is not your project, it is theirs.

31
00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:58.050
You write the five requests down with their impact,

32
00:01:58.201 --> 00:02:00.163
for the sponsor: 10 days together.

33
00:02:01.175 --> 00:02:05.250
Option A: we keep them, and the end date moves two weeks.

34
00:02:05.951 --> 00:02:09.643
Option B: we drop the stock check and the ERP session.

35
00:02:10.672 --> 00:02:13.447
He chooses B, and tells sales himself.

36
00:02:14.461 --> 00:02:18.234
As a junior it feels risky to push back on a senior client.

37
00:02:18.983 --> 00:02:21.293
You don't: you ask the sponsor to choose.

38
00:02:22.305 --> 00:02:25.498
Sometimes the change is right, because the goal was wrong.

39
00:02:26.293 --> 00:02:30.112
Then change the scope officially, with a new goal sentence and plan,

40
00:02:30.263 --> 00:02:31.819
rather than letting it drift.

41
00:02:32.863 --> 00:02:35.441
Formal projects have a change control board;

42
00:02:35.626 --> 00:02:38.877
in most improvement projects the steering group does it.

43
00:02:40.244 --> 00:02:41.765
Watch your own side too.

44
00:02:42.488 --> 00:02:46.203
Gold plating is the team adding extras nobody asked for.

45
00:02:47.223 --> 00:02:49.731
A nicer dashboard, one more analysis.

46
00:02:50.445 --> 00:02:53.719
It feels like service, but it eats the same hours.

47
00:02:54.752 --> 00:02:55.704
Experts differ.

48
00:02:56.449 --> 00:02:59.711
Some count every hour; others keep a reserve for favours,

49
00:02:59.898 --> 00:03:01.988
often about 10 percent of the budget.

50
00:03:02.744 --> 00:03:04.625
A rule of thumb, not a standard.

51
00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:07.292
Either way, ask weekly:

52
00:03:07.454 --> 00:03:10.856
does everything we worked on this week serve the goal sentence?

53
00:03:12.211 --> 00:03:13.175
Now the client.

54
00:03:13.902 --> 00:03:18.302
Scope, time and cost hang together in the iron triangle: if one moves,

55
00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:19.672
something else has to.

56
00:03:20.811 --> 00:03:23.818
Asking for more in the same time, for the same budget,

57
00:03:23.992 --> 00:03:26.778
is asking you to cut quality without saying so.

58
00:03:27.493 --> 00:03:29.676
Don't refuse: make the trade visible.

59
00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:31.972
Small yeses add up.

60
00:03:32.727 --> 00:03:37.161
Five quick extras of a day are a full week, on a 20-day project.

61
00:03:38.189 --> 00:03:43.136
Keep a change log: what was asked, when, how much effort, and what was decided.

62
00:03:43.938 --> 00:03:47.362
Then it's about facts, not about who is being difficult.

63
00:03:48.446 --> 00:03:51.732
On a fixed price the risk of extra effort sits with you.

64
00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:56.067
On a day rate the client pays per day worked and carries it.

65
00:03:56.834 --> 00:04:02.058
So scope creep hurts most on a fixed price: the work grows, the price doesn't.

66
00:04:03.454 --> 00:04:07.610
Fixed price at a grid operator: 20 days, three regions.

67
00:04:08.356 --> 00:04:13.500
In week 3 the sponsor, the project's owner at the client, asks for a fourth.

68
00:04:14.245 --> 00:04:15.743
You're in the data anyway.

69
00:04:16.747 --> 00:04:18.825
You estimate 4 extra days.

70
00:04:19.526 --> 00:04:23.648
First ask what the region is for: is there a board question behind it?

71
00:04:24.409 --> 00:04:26.476
A lighter version is often enough.

72
00:04:27.474 --> 00:04:32.060
Offer options, not a wall: add it for 4 days at the agreed day rate,

73
00:04:32.176 --> 00:04:35.937
swap it for region 3, or one key number in 1 day.

74
00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:38.239
Or later, as a follow-up.

75
00:04:39.019 --> 00:04:40.226
The client chooses.

76
00:04:41.253 --> 00:04:44.678
Check with your engagement manager before you name a price.

77
00:04:45.374 --> 00:04:49.530
You may not be allowed to give days away; your firm carries the cost.

78
00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:53.600
Confirm the choice by email and in the change log.

79
00:04:54.312 --> 00:05:00.036
For bigger items, a change request: work, days and price, and the client signs.

80
00:05:01.036 --> 00:05:02.034
Free extras?

81
00:05:02.737 --> 00:05:06.487
Some give one on purpose, named and logged: this one's on us.

82
00:05:07.194 --> 00:05:09.795
Others: it teaches the client to ask again.

83
00:05:10.507 --> 00:05:13.026
Ask: what would you drop to make room for this?
